'''''Epilobium lanceolatum''''', the '''spear-leaved willowherb''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=27 June 2016 |df=dmy-all}}</ref> is a species in the genus ''[[Epilobium]]'', belonging to the [[Onagraceae]] or "evening primrose" family. It grows between {{Convert abbreviated|30 and 60|cm}} high. This perennial plant has lance-shaped leaves, steadily narrowing to both ends, with long [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]]; 5–7&nbsp;mm, grey to green-blue, with widely toothed margins. The flowers are white fading to pink; blooming from June to August. ''Epilobium lanceolatum'' occurs across western Europe.

==Description==
Easily confused with ''[[Epilobium montanum]]'' and ''[[Epilobium collinum|E.&nbsp;collinum]]''.{{sfn|Strgulc Krajšek|Šegedin|Jogan|2009|p=163}}

==Distribution and habitat==
The only recent confirmed finding in Croatia was on [[Medvednica]].{{sfn|Strgulc Krajšek|Šegedin|Jogan|2009|p=163}}

Very rare. Restricted to light forests on [[silicate]] substrates.{{sfn|Strgulc Krajšek|Šegedin|Jogan|2009|p=163}}

